//RS //! The explicit stack every compiler entry point runs on.
//RS //!
//RS //! Lowering is a recursive descent over user syntax, so the frame budget is
//RS //! per nesting level, never per program size — and the front end already caps
//RS //! that depth: [`crate::Diagnostics::MAX_SOURCE_NESTING`] (256) is the deepest
//RS //! nesting sema and the TIR evaluator accept, deeper source is reported as
//RS //! `E1403`. So the worst case a valid program can demand is bounded
//RS //! arithmetic, not a guess. Measured per level: ~144 KiB for TIR lowering
//RS //! (per method-call level) and ~51 KiB for Cranelift lowering (per expression
//RS //! level).
//RS //!
//RS //! * 256 x 144 KiB = 36 MiB — deepest TIR lowering alone
//RS //! * 256 x 51 KiB = 12.75 MiB — deepest Cranelift lowering alone
//RS //! * 256 x 195 KiB = 48.75 MiB — a program paying both at every level
//RS //!
//RS //! # Why this crate owns it
//RS //!
//RS //! * `jet-sema` — the checked-bundle/effect-facts funnel
//RS //! * `jet-driver` — the compile/check/run funnels, and the loader funnel every
//RS //!   public `Loader::load_entry*` shares
//RS //! * `jet-codegen` — checked TIR lowering, MIR lowering, and MIR optimization
//RS //! * `jet-jit` — its public MIR execution entries
//RS //! Installing at those funnels rather than at their callers is the whole
//RS //! point. Each one is public API, so an embedder holding its own bundle, a
//RS //! test harness, or the LSP reaches the recursive descent on whatever stack it
//RS //! happens to have — and wrapping callers one at a time never converges,
//RS //! because the overflow just moves to the next unwrapped caller.
//RS //!
//RS //! I6 keeps the seam one-directional — `jet-jit` must not depend on
//RS //! `jet-driver`, and `jet-sema` depends on neither — so the flag lives in the
//RS //! deepest crate all four take a path dependency on. That is the same reason
//RS //! the `JitBackend` / `RunOutcome` execution seam lives here rather than in
//RS //! any one side.
//RS 
//RS use std::cell::Cell;
//RS 
//RS thread_local! {
//RS     /// Set on the worker itself, never on the thread that spawned it:
//RS     /// thread-locals do not cross a spawn, so the flag has to be established
//RS     /// by the code that runs with the big stack.
//RS     static ON_COMPILER_WORKER: Cell<bool> = const { Cell::new(false) };
//RS }
//RS 
//RS /// 192 MiB covers the deepest accepted source nesting through sema. Nested
//RS /// ordinary calls recurse through `check_call_inner`; that function's frame
//RS /// is ~680 KiB per level (measured: 96 nested `id(id(...))` overflowed 64 MiB).
//RS /// 256 × 680 KiB = 170 MiB, with room for parser/TIR/Cranelift frames riding
//RS /// along. A thread stack is reserved address space committed page by page, so
//RS /// an ordinary compile still touches only the pages it uses.
//RS ///
//RS /// The budget is recursion depth only; unwind space is not a second term.
//RS /// Both unwind phases run on the panicking thread's own stack, so a raise
//RS /// that runs out of it faults on the guard page and std's per-thread handler
//RS /// says so: `thread '<name>' has overflowed its stack`, then
//RS /// `fatal runtime error: stack overflow` (`std::sys::pal::unix::stack_overflow`).
//RS /// That text is the only abort this number can answer for.
//RS ///
//RS /// `fatal runtime error: failed to initiate panic, error 5` is a different
//RS /// defect and a bigger stack cannot move it. Error 5 is `_URC_END_OF_STACK`
//RS /// (`library/unwind/src/types.rs`), which libgcc's phase-1 loop returns when
//RS /// it walked off the top of the stack without finding a *handler*
//RS /// (`unwind.inc` -> `uw_frame_state_for`): a panic raised where no
//RS /// `catch_unwind` is above it at all. Two sources produce it.
//RS ///
//RS /// The first is a thread-local destructor — glibc runs those from
//RS /// `__call_tls_dtors` after the thread's Rust entry frame and its
//RS /// `catch_unwind` have already returned, which is also why the
//RS /// `extern "C" destroy` shim does not convert it into the "non-unwinding
//RS /// panic" abort: that shim is a phase-2 cleanup pad, and phase 2 never starts.
//RS ///
//RS /// The second is a Cranelift JIT frame. `cranelift-jit` registers no unwind
//RS /// information for the code it emits, so phase 1 cannot walk past one and any
//RS /// outer `catch_unwind` is unreachable even though it exists. That class is
//RS /// fixed at the boundary, not here: `jet-jit`'s `host_seam` generates an
//RS /// `extern "C"` shim per host symbol that catches and converts inside its own
//RS /// C frame (#1997). If you are reading this because you saw error 5, check
//RS /// which of the two you have before touching this number — neither is a
//RS /// stack-space condition, and this constant answers for exactly one abort,
//RS /// the `has overflowed its stack` pair above.
//RS pub const COMPILER_STACK_SIZE: usize = 192 * 1024 * 1024;
//RS 
//RS /// Raising the accepted nesting depth must raise the stack that lowers it.
//RS const _: () = assert!(
//RS     COMPILER_STACK_SIZE >= crate::Diagnostics::MAX_SOURCE_NESTING * 680 * 1024,
//RS     "the compiler worker stack must cover the deepest nesting the front end accepts",
//RS );
//RS 
//RS /// Whether this thread already *is* a compiler worker.
//RS ///
//RS /// Callers that carry thread-local state across the boundary check this first
//RS /// so they skip the capture/restore dance entirely on the inline path.
//RS pub fn on_compiler_worker() -> bool {
//RS     ON_COMPILER_WORKER.with(Cell::get)
//RS }
//RS 
//RS /// Run `work` with the compiler's stack budget.
//RS ///
//RS /// Re-entrant by construction: on a thread that already has the budget the
//RS /// closure runs inline, so the boundary can be installed at *every* public
//RS /// entry without any path ever nesting two workers. That is what removes the
//RS /// whack-a-mole — an entry does not have to know whether some outer entry
//RS /// already crossed.
//RS ///
//RS /// This primitive carries no thread-local state of its own. Each installing
//RS /// crate knows which of its own thread-locals the work reads or publishes and
//RS /// wraps this with exactly that capture/restore: `jet_driver::run_compiler_work`
//RS /// and `Sema::check_bundle_opts_for_output_with_context` carry the comptime
//RS /// ambient hooks; checked TIR is lowered once to MIR, optimized, and handed
//RS /// to the `JitBackend`; and `jet_jit::on_compiler_stack` carries the trace
//RS /// flags, the tier rows and the `core.perf` fidelity signal. Each checks
//RS /// [`on_compiler_worker`] before capturing, so the inline path stays
//RS /// allocation-free.
//RS ///
//RS /// A worker's storage lasts one outermost call, so an installing crate owes the
//RS /// capture/restore not only to state a caller sets up or inspects around the
//RS /// call, but to any thread-local whose contract is *per session* — state a
//RS /// later call on the same caller thread is entitled to read. Such state is
//RS /// lent to the worker and taken back (`jet_jit`'s fidelity signal), or the
//RS /// session owner installs this boundary once around the whole session so every
//RS /// call runs inline on one worker (`jet_jit`'s resident module, live heap and
//RS /// `crate::Persist` store). Making it process-wide instead is not a third
//RS /// option: that leaks one session into a concurrent unrelated one.
//RS ///
//RS /// Values and panics both cross unchanged. The value rides out through
//RS /// `join`; a panic is captured on the worker with `catch_unwind` and re-raised
//RS /// on the caller with `resume_unwind`, so the caller observes the original
//RS /// payload at the original panic location — the ICE path and the diagnostics a
//RS /// caller catches keep their shape instead of being reshaped into an error.
//RS ///
//RS /// The capture sits directly around `work`, not on the join result, so the
//RS /// transport owes nothing to `std::thread::scope`'s internals. The worker never
//RS /// dies panicking, `ScopeData::a_thread_panicked` is never set, and `scope` can
//RS /// never substitute its own `"a scoped thread panicked"` payload for the
//RS /// compiler's. `resume_unwind` then runs on the caller's own frame, outside the
//RS /// scope, so the caller's unwind is raised exactly once, by us.
//RS ///
//RS /// What the boundary cannot move is the panic *hook*: it runs at panic time, on
//RS /// the worker, before any payload crosses back. A hook that reads thread-local
//RS /// state therefore reads the worker's — that is why a compiler panic's message
//RS /// lands outside libtest's per-test output capture, and why
//RS /// `JET_SCHEDULER_CATCHING_PANIC` (`jet-codegen/src/Prelude/Scheduler.rs`) is
//RS /// read on the worker rather than on the caller. `resume_unwind` deliberately
//RS /// does not run the hook a second time.
